B. Covariance Retrieval
The practitioner may wonder how to retrieve the covariance in the original variables. Assume we have a Gaussian vector x ∼ (μ, Σ), and we want to approximate g(x) as a Gaussian. This might addressed resorting to the unscented transform but a more basic and direct approach is as follows. Consider A a matrix and b a vector. Then it is known from probability theory that
Ax + b ∼ (Aμ + b, AΣAT ). (22)
Then, we can write x = μ + e with e ∼ N (0, Σ) and linearizing we find g(x) ≈ g(μ) + ∂g
∂x (μ)e and applying linear
